Audi: "Dieselgate" refurbishment takes longer than planned
"The diesel task force still exists," Peter Merten's chief executive told the "Automobilwoche" industry publication.
The imprisoned and furloughed Audi CEO Rupert Stadler had announced in December 2017 that the task force would be dissolved soon.
"In the first quarter of 2018, all engine/transmission combinations are expected to be tested," he had said at the time.
Mertens now said: "At the beginning of July, we achieved an important milestone in resolving the diesel crisis.
We have completed the internal technical analyses of our VTDI engines.
The taskforce was founded in September 2015 after the discovery of the diesel scandal at Audi parent Volkswagen.
Mertens stressed that the carmaker had made "serious organizational changes" so that such a scandal would never happen again.
The VW supervisory board had adjourned a decision on Stadler's future on Friday and wants to discuss further this Tuesday.
Investigative authorities accuse the Audi CEO of having approved the sale of diesel cars with incorrect exhaust gas values after becoming aware of the exhaust fraud.
He knew about the manipulations or deliberately ignored them.
Bram Schot is currently head of the company’s managing director.
Due to the switch to the new WLTP exhaust gas and consumption testing standard, there are production failures at Audi, Schot told the "Heilbronner Stimme".
In principle, he sees a falling demand for sedans such as the Audi models A6, A7 and A8, which are produced in Neckarsulm.
"The segment itself is difficult," he said.
Schot relies on electromobility.
For the e-tron, the first Audi electric car, there are already 15,000 reservations available.
Grindel seeks re-election - Lahm to join DFB bureau
Mainz (dpa) - Reinhard Grindel wants to run again for office as DFB president despite the criticism of his person after the World Cup exit and the Özil affair next year.
"I'm doing my work and making an effort to do them as well as possible," Grindel said as a guest early Sunday morning at ZDF's "Current Sports Studio."
You have to try again and again for re-election, I will do that.
And then you have to see if the support is there.
According to his own words, the head of the association would find it "strange" if he did not "would like working on the future project" after the surcharge for the European Football Championship 2024.
Grindel has been head of the association since April 2016 as the successor to Wolfgang Niersbach, who was overthrown over the summer fairytale affair.
In the fall of 2016, he was elected for a first complete term of office of three years.
The next vote will take place at the DFB-Bundestag on 27 September 2019.
According to Grindel, the future EM chief organizer Philipp Lahm is to work in the DFB Bureau.
"I would be happy if my colleagues in the Bureau agreed that he will continue to consult in this role in the Bureau in the futurent term and then, of course, is cordially invited to make his experience available on sporting issues," Grindel said.
He did not want to participate in speculations on whether Lahm could become his successor as DFB boss.
As a consequence of the affair about the photos of Mesut Özil and Ilkay Gündogan with the Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdogan, Grindel expressed the wish that a national player without a migrant background should become an integration ambassador of the DFB in the future.
Cacau and Celia Sasic, a native of Brazilia, who currently has familial roots in Cameroon.
"If someone without a migrant background has a clear impact into society and football and says "the topic is important, we have to take care", that would also be an initiative to provide a better foundation for our clubs on the ground, where integration must work," Grindel said.
