Erdoğan opens mosque in Cologne
Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdoğan officially opened the Ditib Mosque in Cologne on Saturday.
Erdoğan had previously met during his three-day state visit with, among others, Chancellor Angela Merkel and German President Frank-Walter Steinmeier.
Criticism of Germany
In his opening speech, Erdoğan called on the German government to extradite supporters of the Gülen movement living in Germany and take action against Kurdish separatists.
We're going to protect our citizens - no matter where they live.
We will not allow sympathizers of the PKK or Gülen movement to shake our citizens and harm German-Turkish friendship," he said.
For the opening of the Ditib Mosque, Cologne's city center had been partially closed.
Several thousand police officers were deployed - including snipers.
The Ditib Islam Association is under criticism because it is considered an extended arm of the Turkish government.
"Global Citizen Festival": music against poverty: stars, booing and scare seconds
Musicians such as Janet Jackson, John Legend, Shawn Mendes and Cardi B have advocated for more engagement in the fight against poverty and disease at a joint concert in New York's Central Park.
"Engage, wherever you can," Mendes said at the festival on the night of Sunday in front of around 60,000 spectators.
Thank you for coming here, that's a dream.
Cardi B appeared in a red outfit with fringes and golden microphone.
I'm nervous, I'm sweating, but it's okay because I'm here ready to change the world.
Actors such as Hugh Jackman and Forest Whitaker, as well as representatives of governments, the United Nations and aid agencies, also appeared on stage at the multi-hour spectacle.
In between, however, there was a scare second, when many spectators considered the sound of a falling metal barrier to be gunshots, got scared and tried to flee.
In a morning, panic arose, people ran into confusion.
Seven spectators were slightly injured in the process, organizer Hugh Evans said.
The New York police and the musician Chris Martin, however, calmed people from the stage.
"No one's trying to injure anyone," Martin said.
You're all safe.
Tickets for the "Global Citizen Festival" concert were available for free on the Internet, as every year.
If you wanted to grab one, you had to read and spread texts, photos and videos about the global fight against poverty and diseases on the Internet.
For the numerous musicians and actors, there were cheers and applause from the audience - booing, on the other hand for Republican Senator Jeff Flake.
Together with his democratic colleague Chris Coons, he advocated for more equality between men and women on stage.
On Friday, however, Flake had spoken in the U.S. Senate Judiciary Committee in favor of Brett Kavanaugh's nomination as a judge on the Supreme Court and thus knocked many women in front of their heads.
At least three women have made allegations of abuse up to the attempted rape against Kavanaugh, which he rejects.
Flake demanded an FBI examination of the allegations, after two women, according to their own according to their own sexual assaults, confronted him in an elevator on the way to the committee meeting.
"You can also come together with me in an elevator at any time," Flake said at the concert in New York.
Press freedom: Federal government defends expulsion of journalist
Government spokesman Steffen Seibert has defended the ejection of a journalist from Chancellor Angela Merkel's (CDU) press conference and Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdoğan.
In the chancellery, the Turkish journalist, who lives in Hamburg, had covered a T-shirt with the Turkish and German inscription "Freedom for Journalists" and had gone into the front row.
The T-shirt, was not the problem, Seibert told Bild am Sonntag.
It was about a disruption of the expiration of the press conference.
As a result of the action of the journalist, there was a strong unrest, which was unacceptable.
That’s why he caused personal protectors to take the man out.
Seibert said that it was not allowed to demonstrate political concerns at press conferences in the Federal Chancellery.
This applies completely regardless of whether it is a legitimate concern or not.
The rules are the rules.
There are a number of journalists in prison in Turkey.
Many of them are accused of alleged espionage activities or support or membership in prohibited parties or the Fethullah-Gülen movement.
The Turkish leadership blames the movement for the failed coup attempt on July 15, 2016.
Some of the accused journalists face several decades imprisonment.
Foreign journalists are also not safe from the Turkish authorities: only at the beginning of September, the Austrian journalist Max Zirngast was arrested.
Most recently, the cases of imprisoned journalists Meşale Tolu and Deniz Yücel had caused a stir.
Although they were released and were able to return to Germany, trials are still ongoing in Turkey against the two.
Munich: Florian David Fitz completely drunk with Ina Müller
His fans usually know him so fresh and handsome: Florian David Fitz.
You have to look twice: Yes, that's really Florian David Fitz.
