A beer with Ina Müller.
Treysa struggles with vandalism and debris
The entrance to the 36-kilometre-long railway cycle path in Rotkäppchenland in Treysa is difficult to find and litter.
Recipes against rampant vandalism are being sought, but solutions are hardly emerging.
Shards, cigarette butts, rubbish and rubbish, demolished trash cans and meanwhile dismantled signs - this is how the entrance with a seating group to the cycle path on the Haaße-Hügel in Treysa has been presented for a long time.
For the exemplary object Bahnradweg in Rotkäppchenland a more than inglorious sight.
The topic concerns members of the ADFC local group Schwalmstadt but also others for a long time, according to the cycling officer of the city Ulrich Wüstenhagen.
The problem had already been raised in the cycling advisory board.
So far, there is no solution.
The proposals ranged from the installation of the seating group to regular emptying of the waste bins and cleaning of the areas at the entry point.
"I think the latter would be the order of the day - regardless of possible further activities," says Wüstenhagen.
Guido Hahn (ADFC) reports that he has also submitted the topic to the local advisory board.
Not only the rubbish drives cyclists and local residents around - the seating group is a regular meeting place to celebrate on weekends.
It is not only often noisy, in the occasions it is also destroyed: posts torn from the earth, trash cans demolished, signs bent over, seating furniture smeared.
Volunteers would already have cleaned up more often, says ADFC member Susanne Klippert.
However, the actions are only the proverbial drop on the hot stone.
They are annoyed that the rest area on the cycle path in Treysa is a meeting place for drinking.
"Along the entire cycle path is clearly the focal point here at Haaße-Hügel," confirms the cycling commissioner, who is now also worried about the new cycling site in Ziegenhain, which will be opened today.
“If she suffers the same fate, it becomes really expensive.”
Another idea is video surveillance on the hill: "Possibly this puts the threshold much higher to get snuggled down on common property.
However, this would presuppose that a police patrol comes by in a timely manner.
In principle, Wüstenhagen wants a development of the Haaße hill.
He had to be made more attractive.
So far you can be very satisfied with the frequency of the cycle path.
The tourist marketing is still expandable.
Our cycle path is very high-quality compared to others in Germany," according to the ADFC members.
As an extremely critical point of danger, ADFC member Susanne Klippert assesses the situation on Ascheröder Straße in Treysa.
There's no turn-of-turning lane on Haaße hills.
In busy traffic, cyclists have to wait in the middle of the road between two-sided traffic flow," she explains.
This is not a problem for sporty drivers, but for the elderly and especially for children.
Music contest in the Kulturbahnhof: The "Kö von Hiltrup" and the lake
The first call to the music contest was a success, creative and togetherness.
" To present a song for Hiltrup" is the appeal of the district offensive Hiltrup, in the "music contest for big and small, young and experienced musicians".
A contest "with twinkles in the sympathetic Hiltruper family," as Karl Franke put it.
In fact, it was soon a familial cooperation with coffee and cake from the volunteers of the Kulturbahnhof.
Seven formations competed, from the one-man band to the large ensemble: Alvin Jasic with pianist Marcel Untied, Henk Humberg with guitar, Rolf Ihmig, a long-living in Hiltrup, the Nine from the "Hiltruper Sternfarben", three members and three generations of the Trenkamp and Claus families, an ensemble from the kindergarten Lummerland and finally Rike
This contest, district mayor Joachim Schmidt explained, is a novelty.
We haven't experienced that in other neighborhoods before.
What did the lyricists relate to?
At the villages, Lake Hiltrup and Marktallee were often mentioned frequently.
Rolf Ihmig named the avenue "Kö von Hiltrup" in his hit-like song.
The Kulturbahnhof has also been honoured.
Again and again it was about the experience of togetherness in these places, especially with Alvin Jasic.
Karl Franke revived the short conversion breaks with short interviews with the performers or hints about their work.
This is how Humberg learned that he had actually studied chemistry, but now also teaches guitar.
From the sound engineer Stefan Schemmelmann Franke knew to report that he recently appeared in Ludwigshafen - at a large event of BASF with Barclay James Harvest in the program.
And Alvin Jasic, meanwhile, has placed all sorts of things on Youtube.
"It's not about winning," Franke had said.
In fact, all participants received a gift from Hiltrup gastronomy.
Nevertheless, there was something special for the favorites of the jury - so that others can hear their songs as well.
Alvin Jasic and his pianist Marcel Untied with sound engineer Schemmelmann can record a whole CD.
Other musicians can record a song: Ihmig with his Hiltrup-Schlager, who particularly impressed Franke with his musicality, and the "Lummerland-Rudel": "a very nice performance" with ten children and two educators on guitar and cajon, Laura Kastner and Lina Tölle.
Like everyone, the children performed again for the final - and sounded "really good" even without electronic support, as Franke said.
The jury was guided by the aspects of originality, reference to Hiltrup, catchiness of the melody and recognition value.
Gisbert Keller from the Westphalian School of Music, Andreas Klomfass from the KvG-Gymnasium and Oliver Ahlers from the Wirtschaftsverbund as well as Jan Kaven, chairman of the district offensive, belonged to the jury.
The district offensive had launched the event on the occasion of its tenth anniversary.
She regarded the event as a complete success.
Everyone was "very satisfied", and "the atmosphere was good" in the soon very full cultural station.
The word game: "Decrypto" in the game test
The players form two teams sitting opposite each other.
For each side there is a sight screen, in it four cards with keywords, which you - the old trick with the red foil - can only be deciphered when you have put them in it.
In this way, it is ensured that the other side cannot read anything by mistake.
On the maps, for example, you can read from left to right: "1 Renaissance.
2nd windows.
3. Cubes.
4. Feast".
These are the terms that need to be encrypted from now on.
Because it is not the terms, but codes that should be guessed here.
Alternately one player per team is the encryptor.
He gets a code card, on which maybe "3.4.2" says.
"Decrypto" should not guess the terms behind the secret film, but their encryption codes.
His task in this case: in this order and in this example - he should describe the terms "cube", "feast" and "window" - in such a way that his own team recognizes the correct order and the opposing team as far as possible; because that wins the game if he manages to crack the code twice.
The encryptor therefore notes and reads aloud: "Throwing" for the dice, "lots" as a contrast to the festival; here, of course, something like "party" would also be possible and "Prague" because he paid attention at the school and knows the window fall there.
That's how it goes round by lap, team by team continue.
You don't know the terms of the other side - but you can rhyme it better with every hint that the encryptor gives there.
As mysterious as possible, but not too crazy, the circumscriptions should be, otherwise you make it unnecessarily difficult for your team.
The most cryptical thing about "Decrypto" is unfortunately the rulebook.
The players probably have to study it quite long and in detail until they can finally play.
The explanation video at decrypto.info on the net makes it easier.
But those who have started will be fascinated by how much feeling is in this inconspicuous game.
Overall, however, "Decrypto" is not quite as conclusive as its predecessor and genre primus "code names", which also puts an agent topic on a game of fewer words, but is much better to play in small groups.
And if it's all about guessing and not victory points, you can easily enlarge a version for two.
Kulmbach: Man shows police Hitler salute and harasses guests in the pub
A 48-year-old man employed the Kulmbach police inspection twice in the night from Saturday to Sunday.
First he showed the Hitler salute in the upper city of a passing police patrol, later he also stood out in a pub in the upper city when he bothered other guests.
After he did not comply with the issued referral by the police, he had to spend the night at the Kulmbach police inspection in the detention cell.
In a sober state, he then also has to answer for the use of license plates of unconstitutional organizations.
Betriebswerk Pressig was home to the push locomotives
For decades, the Raid operating plant in Pressig was the turntable in the
In 1991 a piece of railway history ended.
"When buffers slammed at buffers, you could hear it in the whole pressing," recalls Georg Dinkel, who archives the history of the place and the train station.
That didn't upset anyone.
That's when the brakes squeaked too and rattled the wagons.
That was pure railroad.
Statements that symbolize Dinkel’s home Pressig.
Nowhere in the Frankenwald was a place so shaped by the railways.
Here was the starting point of the Steilrampen mountain route to the north, which after an ascent of about 220 vertical meters has its apex in Steinbach am Wald.
In order to cope with this, train and push locomotives were needed in the past, which had their home in Pressig.
In 1901, the Bavarian state parliament approved the construction of a train workshop with 21 locomotive sheds, apprentice workshop, overhead line and railway master's workshop.
"The centerpiece was the hub, which had a diameter of 18 meters after a reconstruction in 1899," says Norbert Heidrich from Hirschaid, railway historian and for 47 years professional railroaders.
Locomotives were also operated by the railway works plant (BW) Pressig, which was subordinated to Bamberg in 1968, for the branch lines Pressig - Tettau, Ludwigsstadt - Lehesten, Stockheim - Sonneberg and Kronach - Nordhalben.
On July 15, 1950, the "Volksblatt" reported a staff of 160 men; 13 steam locomotives and eight Elloks were stationed in Pressig at that time.
The electrification of the line in 1939 already minimized the need for train and push locomotives.
From 1977, they were only needed for freight trains.
Until 1991, at least 10 000 wagons were still ranked per month.
In 1982, parts of the factory were demolished, in 1991 the rest was demolished.
Heidrich: "It is unfortunate that one has not envisaged a better use of the site, also in terms of freight transport."
At Pentecost 1989, the Nuremberg Railway Directorate celebrated a pompous station festival in Pressig on the occasion of the jubilee "50 Years of Electrification Nuremberg - Saalfeld".
"There were 10 000 enthusiastic spectators here," recalls Georg Dinkel.
A little later the end.
On 1 June 1991, the last 30 men were withdrawn, the shunting station Pressig was history.
"The shunters and inhibitors did the most difficult work in train traffic," recalls Dinkel.
Any mishandling would have fatal consequences.
Why the station Pressig-Rothenkirchen led for the reversal of the hierarchy of the two places and many more historical and current pictures can be found here in the infrankenPlus article.
