As part of the series "Art and Pleasure", a special exhibition was opened in Mittelehrenbach over the weekend.
Organizers and visitors registered the ideal weather with great relief when the special vernissage "Windspiel-Performance" was opened in the outdoor area of the Dahlman-Schmitz estate, Mittelehrenbach.
It takes place under the umbrella of the tourism association "Rund ums Walberla" as part of the series "Art and Pleasure".
It opened its doors in the 15th year.
A large number of visitors showed the fascination of the exhibition.
21 specimens of 20 artists can now be visited in Mittelehrenbach.
Owner Ernst Jürgen Dahlmann welcomed the artists to the opening of the vernissage.
He has been there since the very beginning.
As well as Volker Hahn, who made a name for himself as the main organizer and artist alike.
Somewhat surprisingly, Hahn announced his resignation a few days ago.
With Tania Engelke and Kurt Neubauer, however, he placed his task in proven, young hands with a good prospect for the future.
In the past, both have already been actively involved in the selection of artists and co-created flyers.
Once again this year there is a 70-cent special stamp designed by them on the occasion of Kunst & Genuss, available at each art station.
There you can also purchase this year's poster "Betzn austanzen", designed by Volker Hahn, for eight euros.
Hahn was pleased about the high popularity of the special exhibition.
For the musical framing Max Marcus Stadler from Nuremberg could be won.
Walking through the spacious garden, it quickly becomes clear to the visitor that the title "Windriggle Performance" formally challenges the artistic imagination and ultimately expresses it playfully.
They are artworks made of metal or ceramic, often provided with color accents.
Already at the entrance of the estate awaits the visitor on the right side the "Glücksbaum" by the artist Werner Baur, Eschenau.
It is a tree stump, brightened in color, playfully provided with 20 windmills, 24 horseshoes and 24 aluminum tubes as a sound piece.
With the sun flashing through the bushes in the background, the object looks fairytale and invites you to linger.
Barbara Waschneck comes from Eckental, who is preferably dedicated to the event 15 years of K&G in three-dimensional letters.
There are a lot of parts, in the wind in motion.
Siegrid Frey from Eckental exhibited her work "The Wind the Heavenly Child" halfway.
And at the end of the area, Volker Hahn shows his colorful fantasy structure made of clay, cement and stainless steel.
The theme "Windspile Performance" for strolling through the versatile art exhibition.
