50 years of MGV 68 Albachten: Singers celebrate 50th birthday festively
1968 - this was the time of the protest and the student movement.
And the Albachteners also vented their displeasure at that time.
At least five of them.
They didn't want to come to terms with the fact that there should be no more singing life in the district after the dissolution of the church choir "Cäcilia".
It has now become a legend: At a bank in the Tinnenbusch, they launched the MGV 68 Albachten.
That was 50 years ago.
Now the jubilee was celebrated big in the "House of Encounter".
"It's going to be full today," Chairman Jürgen Holsteige suspected, and he should be right.
There were 150 guests, a real success for the Albachten singers.
Especially for the anniversary, they had revived the foundation festival.
For years it was tradition, then there was a pause.
Just in time for the 50-year anniversary, the traditional festival was back.
An elegant ball it became, festive clothes had been asked beforehand.
As usual, the guests did not sit in the "house of encounters" in rows of seats, but comfortably at tables.
In between, there was movement: DJ Matthias Drees from Nottuln put on the knobs and asked on the dance floor.
A matter of honor that the singers of the MGV 68 Albachten themselves sounded the voice.
In keeping with the occasion, cheerful songs were announced.
Almost set: "Such a day, as beautiful as today".
Matching the exquisite vine syrup that many had in front of them, there was the "Rüdesheimer wine".
Alluding to another cool drink, "Humulus lupulus," which is the Latin term for "hops."
Once again, the choral direction of Richard Homann, who is one of the fives who were in Tinnenbusch at the time.
"It's just fun to sing under his direction," Holstiege said: "He has such an easy way, it's a feat he's accomplishing." By the way, this time didn't just introduce himself to men.
The women's choir "Hohes C" was also a guest, whose repertoire mainly includes evergreens from the 20s to the 60s.
After all, there was poetry.
Singer Theo Weber had been sitting at his desk until three o'clock in the morning to create his own rhymes.
And Andreas Winterhoff also presented well-sounding poetry.
There was also a large raffle.
With the proceeds, the children of the Albatros Youth Center are to be financed a visit to the all-weather zoo.
