About her marriage to Hans von Borsody: Heide Keller never felt well enough for her husband
On the "dream ship" Heide Keller became a star.
Now she speaks openly that the series may have had a complicity in her marriage with Hans von Borsody.
Her role of the chief hostess Beatrice von Ledebur on the "dream ship" made Heide Keller (76) famous, but on 1 January 2018 she disembarked forever.
The series, which made her a star, could be partly responsible for her failed marriage to Hans von Borsody (1929 - 2013), with which she was married for ten years.
"That could have been a reason, too.
Because I grew up with this work, traveled around the world alone with a suitcase and many colleagues admired by me perceived me on an eye-level basis," Keller says in an interview with Bild am Sonntag.
At first, she had only ever been "the wife of Hans von Borsody", but that had changed with the "dream ship".
Because now the journalists only called at Heide Keller.
“I don’t know if he liked it so much.”
She thinks it's possible that her then-husband may well have been envious, but "like many men Hans also shunned conversations about problems and feelings."
Keller had always tried for Hans von Borsody "to be the only right and he didn't want to get to know my fears, my weaknesses.
That means in good German: I've disguised myself," as she further explains.
She has always had the feeling of "not being good enough for him.
He saw in me an emancipated woman, which I really never was, even though I sometimes seemed cheeky and courageous on stage.
I wanted our love to be forever.”
After a previously failed marriage to Thomas Härtner, Keller said yes again, "although I was afraid that it could also go wrong a second time".
The day before the civil ceremony I sat howling in the cloakroom and wanted to cancel everything.
Borsody's marriage also failed.
Why didn't it work out with the men?
Because I wanted too much.
At that time, Keller imagined love "as in the film.
Unfortunately, this vision is not suitable for everyday use.
“I’ve approached love too naïvely.”
The night butterfly, which regularly explores the city’s nightlife in the NZZ resort of Zurich, has ventured to the ZFF opening party this time.
