Elon Musk, founder of the electric car maker Tesla, has escaped his looming dismissal as head of the multibillion-dollar company.
He and the U.S. stock market regulator SEC filed out-of-court litigation over misleading Twitter news in which Musk could have lost permission to lead listed companies.
As part of the settlement, Musk and Tsela pay 20 million dollars each, in addition, Musk relinquishes for three years the office of chairman, a position comparable to the German supervisory board chief.
He is allowed to remain chief executive against it.
In the settlement, which has yet to be upheld by a court, neither Musk nor Tesla admitted wrongdoing.
The SEC had sued the world-renowned manager in the U.S. federality of Manhattan for fraud.
Musk, who also presides over space company SpaceX, is the public face of the loss-writing electric car pioneer, which is now valued at more than 50 billion dollars on the stock market.
Musk isn't aware of any guilt
Musk explained that he had done nothing wrong and was sad and disappointed with the lawsuit.
He had always acted in the sense of truth, transparency and investors.
On August 7, Musk had written to his more than 22 million Twitter supporters that he would perhaps take the company off the stock market and pay 420 dollars per share in the process, the financing was secured.
Tesla would have been valued at a total of 72 billion dollars.
Musk later rowed back: Tesla remains on the stock market.
After the agreement, Tesla will now have to find an external chairman, hire two independent board members, and also set up a committee that monitors Musk's communications.
