Polls: 46 percent don't want Merkel as chancellor by 2021
According to the quarrels of the past few weeks, the GroKo collects several polling low strikes.
The SPD falls behind the AfD in a questioning - Angela Merkel is viewed critically.
Berlin - The AfD has overtaken the SPD in the Emnid Sunday trend for the Picture on Sunday nationwide for the first time nationwide.
In the weekly survey of the opinion research institute, the AfD increases by one counter to 17 percent, the SPD gives up one point compared to the previous week and lands at 16 percent.
CDU and CSU also lose a counter and only come to 27 percent.
That's the deepest value so far for the Union in the Sunday trend.
The Greens reach 15 per cent, the Left 11 and the FDP 10 per cent.
According to Emnid, only 43 percent of the votes would be received by the SPD, CDU and CSU together.
As a result, the grand coalition has currently lost the majority on the Sunday question at all major research institutes - with the exception of Allensbach.
The ruling parties consistently get 43 to 45 percent of the votes (Allensbach: 51 percent).
In the 2017 federal election, together, they still received 53.4 percent.
For the other parties, the range is also large: The AfD, in several polls the second strongest party, comes to 15 to 18 percent.
The Greens reach vacines of 12.5 to 17 percent.
The left is an average of 10 percent, FDP at 9 percent.
For statistical reasons, such polls, depending on the share of the vote, have an uncertainty up and down from 2 to 3 percent.
But the support for Chancellor Angela Merkel (CDU) in the population has also dropped significantly since the 2017 federal election.
Currently, only 48 percent of citizens find that Merkel is the right person in the chancellery, as the Emnid survey on behalf of the BamS revealed.
According to the poll, only one in two (50 percent) currently wants the grand coalition to hold until the next regular federal election in three years, 41 percent are for a new election.
Even in a further poll published on Saturday, there had been significant losses of popularity for Merkel.
The Germans also judge divided whether Merkel should run again for the CDU chairmanship in December.
47 percent are in favour, according to Emnid, 44 per cent against it.
Among Union supporters, 64 percent want a candidacy, 35 percent do not.
