Allbrighd Foundation report: Women remain a rarity on corporate boards
Thus, only 8 percent of the boards of directors were female as of September 1, according to the report "The power of monoculture", which is officially presented by the Allbright Foundation this Monday and was submitted in advance to the German Press Agency.
"The companies will have to position themselves differently, otherwise the international competitors will move past them," foundations managing director Wiebke Ankersen said.
In the USA, Great Britain or Sweden, purely male-occupied management teams - with a few exceptions - are no longer conceivable.
By comparison, the backlog makes German competitors less diverse, innovation-friendly and future-oriented.
It's a different corporate culture.
In addition, the slight increase in the proportion of women among German stock exchange companies was due solely to positive stimulus among companies in the medium-sized index (MDax).
According to Allbright data, only 6.8 percent of board members are female.
In the previous year, however, the figure was 3.8 percent.
Among the 30 Dax companies - in recent years pioneers - the proportion of women stagnated.
Most recently, 26 women worked on the boards.
"I'm sure the Dax companies will be recruiting consistent women again in the coming year - in their own interest," Ankersen said.
Only RWE and Heidelberg Cement had signalled that they would continue to make no self-commitment to increase the "zero percent" share so far.
A different picture emerges from the supervisory boards: As of September 1, the proportion of women there was 30.5 percent - 45 women more and 68 men less than a year ago.
"Recruiting just as many women for the boards each year, a share of 40 percent would be reached as early as 2023," the report said.
