VW core brand is missing: Paris car salon in the wake of diesel debate
Every two years, the Paris Motor Show takes place alternately with the IAA in Frankfurt.
Paris - Officially no issue and yet ubiquitous: The diesel scandal and the struggle for driving bans and retrofits are unlikely to unleash the German car companies at the industry meeting in Paris.
At the Automobilsalon 2018, they will show their innovations from next week together with the competition from all over the world in the French capital.
The diesel no longer plays a major role.
However, the possible exchange and retrofitting offers for diesel owners in Germany, which are still to be negotiated with the government in Berlin until then, are likely to be an important topic at least behind the brilliant backdrops of the exhibition stands.
A big name is missing in Paris: The Volkswagen Group is there with Audi, Porsche and other subsidiaries, but not with the core brand VW itself.
Audi and Mercedes-Benz are there and, among other things, have their first pure electric models: The e-tron of the Ingolstädter is to be launched at the end of the year, the EQC of Stuttgart 2019.
Both have already been officially presented.
BMW’s iNext is not scheduled to launch until 2021.
The focus in Paris is on the new three.
A total of more than 250 exhibitors will be represented this year from 4 to 14 October.
In addition to car companies, motorcycle manufacturers as well as numerous mobility service and software providers are also present in their own halls.
The fair, which celebrates its 120th birthday this year, takes place every two years alternately with the IAA in Frankfurt.
In 2016, there were about one million visitors.
At least 380 people have died in the severe earthquakes and subsequent tsunami on the Indonesian island of Sulawesi.
Dozens are still missing.
There are also more than 500 injured.
The island was shaken by two earthquakes on Friday.
The first quake had a magnitude of 5.9, the second the magnitude 7.4.
Communication and transport connections are damaged, there was enormous property damage.
After the quakes, the tsunami broke over the west coast of Sulawesi.
Hundreds had gathered on the beach for a party in the coastal town of Palu.
It is feared that casualties figures are still rising sharply.
So far, the figures have only come from Palu.
However, numerous other communities are also affected - along Sulawesi's west coast.
About 150 bikers celebrate church service in Werdohl
Motorcycle pastor Frank Schröder (right) from Herne celebrated the closing service for the motorcyclists in the Friedenskirche in Eveking on Sunday with a team of helpers.
Werdohl - The square in front of the Peace Church in Eveking belonged to motorcyclists on Sunday.
After all, the closing service for the bikers was on the program.
Frank Schröder is responsible for the motor pastor within the Evangelical Church of Westphalia and is thus responsible for the pastoral concerns of horse-powered two-wheelers.
Between April and September, the traditional motorcycle service (MoGo) takes place at Haus Nordhelle on the last Sunday of a month.
Bikers traditionally celebrate the conclusion of the season at the Peace Church in Eveking.
Around 150 bikers had come together there on Sunday to celebrate an anything but ordinary service.
As early as 10 a.m., the cars were lined up in front of the church entrance portal.
Initially, the biker breakfast was on the program and it could be strengthened with coffee and cake or even with a hearty curry sausage.
In addition to the MoGo team, the Holy Riders, a Christian motorcycle group from Gummersbach, were also present.
The sermon was given during the biker service by Father Dirk Grzegorek.
Singer-songwriter Valerie Lill provided the musical accompaniment.
"Although the bikers prefer blues rock, Valerie Lill strikes rather poetic-quiet tones," says Father Frank Schröder.
This, too, was certainly a novelty in the history of the motorcycle service yesterday.
