Geisingen: Geisinger Christians Celebrate Holy Cross-Feast
Geisinger Catholics celebrate the Holy Cross Festival on September 14 or Sunday after.
The festive service takes place in the city church, then is a procession to the Holy Cross church with the closing blessing.
The Holy Cross Church is home to the Shot Christ, who comes from the 30-year-old war and who, according to legend, is said to have been shot by a Swedish soldier.
Blood flowed from the gunshot wound.
From this, a great pilgrimage developed into the shot Christ, which then also led to the construction of the Holy Cross Church.
This has not yet been completed, only the altar room stands, the missing longhouse is indicated by the trees.
In his sermon, Father Adolf Buhl recalls that the cross is a sign of hope, consolation and life.
The service was poorly attended despite the importance for the Geising parish.
After the service, the procession to the Holy Cross Church took place and with the concluding blessing ended the ecclesiastical part of the cross festival.
For many years, the Catholic church choir still invites the secular part of the festival to the Catholic parish home.
The choir members provided the visitors with savoury dishes as well as a large selection of cakes and adapted to the weather, farm ice cream.
In the afternoon, the parish community still offered guided tours around the city church, Bernd Bendschneider and Petra Schmid-Seger informed visitors on behalf of the parish about the damage to the façade, the roof, the sandstone works and windows as well as the upcoming construction measures, which will continue until next year.
Since the scaffolding has been around for some time, the damage could be determined in more detail and the individual tenders could be prepared more concretely.
It is certain that the renovation will be more expensive than planned, because the damage already present a few years ago has become greater, but it has also been found more, which was only made possible by the inspection by scaffolding.
The plant plastering work has been completed, before the winter the natural stone work is to be started and one hopes to be able to complete a good half of this work this year.
Inauguration: New dormitory in Neu-Ulmer Münsterblickstraße
Lebenshilfe Donau-Iller is opening a new dormitory for people with disabilities in Münsterblickstraße.
The fact that "a very special house" will now be handed over to its destination in Münsterblickstraße - at the inauguration date, all those present agreed.
After all, the twelve wheelchair users who have been living in the dormitorism of Lebenshilfe Donau-Iller for some time are dependent on the special architecture of the new building.
Extensive corridors, corridors and doors, larger rooms and barrier-free entrances for all areas has been taken into account by architect Rudolf Bader in his concept.
The initial planning began about eight years ago, when the former U.S. Army officer's building was put to the scrutiny for the residents with Handycap: "The house had gotten old and was no longer up-to-date in the equipment," Bader explained retrospectively.
In 2016, the diggers approached to demolish the house.
The new building should be designed as friendly and bright as possible, so that the residents can feel comfortable in it, according to the architect.
Lebenshilfe Donau-Iller opens new dormitory
Lebenshilfe invested around 4.6 million euros in the new building.
The Free State contributed 2.4 million euros, the district of Swabia 450000 euros.
According to architect Bader, the construction work has remained within the planned framework - in terms of time and finance.
The managing director of Lebenshilfe, Jürgen Heinz, also thanked him for this.
The balancing act between a barrier-free and at the same time fire-safe construction was not always easy.
Nevertheless, the planners and craftsmen had built a home with the many specifications, which allows the residents to experience the freedom to move even without foreign help.
Gertrud Kreutmayer, head of the social administration of the district of Swabia, commented similarly: "The house is based on the special needs of its residents, who need aids to be mobile".
In doing so, the comfort and functionality had been well combined, Kreutmayer said.
"The property in this location is a real stroke of luck, because it can no longer be acquired in today's situation".
In total, the new dormitory in Münsterblickstraße offers almost 1500 square meters of space for 30 people with disabilities, which are divided into three residential groups.
Lebenshilfe Managing Director Heinz is proud of the eleven full-time employees, all of whom are employed according to tariff.
Germany: Schwesig laments differences between East and West
Berlin - Ahead of Unity Day, the deputy SPD federal chairman Manuela Schwesig appealed to all compatriots not to come to terms with the disadvantage of East Germans.
There are differences that hurt after almost 30 years of unity.
We must not be satisfied with these differences," the Prime Minister of Mecklenburg-Vorpommern told the "Tagesspiegel am Sonntag".
The goal must remain to create equal living conditions throughout the country.
It is unacceptable that East Germans worked longer on average than the people in West Germany, but got 15 percent less salary.
"I can't explain that to anyone almost 30 years after German unity," Schwesig said.
North North Korea: Does this cute dog end the Korean conflict?
The one-year-old Pungsan hunting dog is said to be smoothing the waves between the two Koreas.
In an effort to relax relations with Seoul, Pyongyang has gifted South Korean President Moon Jae in two North Korean Pungsan hunting dogs.
As Moon's office announced on Sunday, the two about one-year-old dogs already reached their recipient on Thursday.
They passed the border town of Panmunjom together with three kilograms of dog food in order to "facilitate familiarization" with the animals.
The dogs with the thick white fur, pointed ears and brown eyes are considered loyal and intelligent.
They belong to the national cultural property of the Democratic People's Republic of Korea.
The North had announced the gift of friendship at the third summit this year between Moon and North Korean leader Kim Jong Un in mid-September in Pyongyang.
The two countries also agreed to compete together at international sports competitions such as the 2020 Olympic Games and apply together to host the Olympic Games in 2032.
In the course of the mutual rapprochement, Kim also agreed to close the Tongchang Ri test facility for rocket propulsions.
