New Week, New Opportunity for the Government
Back to material work - that's the unofficial motto for the Grand Coalition's working week starting today.
Hardly an interview, hardly a speech in which the chancellor and her ministers from the CDU, SPD and also from the CSU did not invoke the "return to material work" mantra-like.
This may have tempted some craftsmen, doctors, cashiers and otherwise employed citizens in this country to the legitimate question of what is actually the difference between work and work, but be it.
If the federal government is now ready to dedicate itself again to its mandate - governing - it should not be stopped now with term debates.
Whether Union and SPD can actually achieve something together after the provocations and embarrassments of the last few weeks will probably already show Monday.
In the evening, the leaders of the ruling parties come together for the coalition committee.
Chancellor Angela Merkel now wants to convene this round regularly to defuse conflicts early; a lesson from all this dispute.
Initially, however, with the diesel retrofits and the Skills Immigration Act, conflictual issues are once again on the agenda.
CSU Transport Minister Scheuer wants to let the car companies get away with exchange premiums, SPD and CDU see the manufacturers in the obligation to technically retrofit older diesel.
While a rapprochement has loomed over the diesel crisis over the weekend, Union and SPD are still far apart from the planned Skilled Immigration Act.
The SPD calls for a stay-at-home perspective for rejected asylum seekers who work or make an apprenticeship.
The Union is strongly opposed to the "change" from the asylum system to the labour market.
So there's going to be strife again among the coalition partners - and how should it be otherwise?
It is not the same and the same that has come together with GroKo, but three partners with very different social images and priorities.
Dispute is good.
Without dispute, a society marked by so diverse, sometimes contradictory interests does not advance.
However, in order to be constructive, Streit must not again be exaggerated for party-tactical reasons on the question of being or nothingness.
It's still about politics here, not theater.
Union and SPD should argue - but always keep an eye on the reality of life of the people.
What is doable and benefits many?
With this leading question, both the diesel and lane change questions can be clarified pragmatically - and thus regain trust.
This is especially why the popular parties have to fight now.
