Police tear up a street in search of women's body
Marxgrün - The police did not want to comment for a long time.
Now there's confirmation.
Since early Saturday morning, dug on the state road between Naila and Bad Steben.
The track is still closed in the Marxgrün area until Monday.
Preceded that investigators had previously deployed an archaeology dog.
The dog, specially trained for the smell of old human bones, together with its guide, was finally able to limit the relevant place to a few meters.
In addition, together with scientists from the Technical University of Munich and the top Bavarian monument protection authority in the area of the said road section, a soil anomaly was discovered.
Prosecutors and the Criminal Policy Court have been handling the missing persons case for more than a year.
Again and again, a cycle path was closed at Marxgrün and examined with sonar devices, because the investigators suspect that Heike’s body may have been buried here in the course of the new construction of the state road 30 years ago.
Paint powder and eggs: riots in Barcelona ahead of Catalonia anniversary
Monday marks the first anniversary of the controversial independence referendum in Catalonia.
But there are riots even before that.
The separatists did not forget the police operation of autumn 2017.
Barcelona - Shortly before the anniversary of the Catalan independence referendum of 1 October 2017, there have been clashes between separatists and the police in central Barcelona.
Catalan security forces were attacked by independence supporters, among other things, when they tried to prevent them from approaching a demonstration by the national police and the civilian guard.
Around 3000 officers from the Policía Nacional and Guardia Civil police units wanted to honor their colleagues with a rally who were on duty at the polling stations last year in the illegal referendum.
During the attempt to prevent citizens from voting, there was also violence at that time.
Catalan police would have formed a chain to keep the demonstrations of the separatists and central security forces separate, Spanish media reported.
Some of the approximately 6000 separatists, however, would then have violently tried to break through the police chain, and pelted the officers, among other things, with paint bags and eggs.
Six people were arrested and 24 others were slightly injured, it said, citing the authorities.
Monday marks the anniversary of the independence referendum for the first time.
After the vote and a subsequent decision to secede the prosperous region of Spain, the central government in Madrid put Catalonia under compulsory administration in the autumn of 2017.
Carles Puigdemont, then's regional president, fled into exile in Belgium.
Other politicians also dropped out abroad.
Several ministers of the then regional government and other activists of the separatist movement, on the other hand, are still in custody.
In December, new elections were helded in the region.
Following the inauguration of the new Catalan government in May, the forced administration was terminated.
But the new regional president Quim Torra also insists on the "right to self-determination" of the Catalans.
