Smears with a local political message: No successful advertising for skate plant
In Lengerich, unknown persons have advertised for the construction of a skate facility in arguably conducive ways.
In several places, there are corresponding smears, says Jürgen Kohne of the city administration at the request of the WN.
In at least one case, criminal charges have been filed with the police for damage to property.
In large letters, the three words "Skatepark für Lengerich!" emblazoned on signs, among other things, at the Gempt-Halle and at the Old Town Hall.
Michael Rottmann, manager of the hall, reports that a corresponding lettering had already been discovered on the advertising sign at the staircase to the Gempt-Bistro after the professional election fair on 19 September.
Since the short message at that time was apparently "only" left with an Edding, it could easily be removed.
"A few days later, however, it stood there again, this time sprayed on," says Rottmann.
He assumes that the corresponding advertising sign must be replaced.
A special note is given precisely by the fact that several large companies from Lengerich are also mentioned on the board.
If the skate system is built, its realization should also be made possible by sponsors.
There are already commitments, explains Jörg Hesselmann from the municipal department of school, sports and culture, without mentioning names.
However, it is quite possible that one or more of the sponsors are identical to those companies that are on the smeared board.
For years, local politics and administration have been dealing with the topic of skating.
After the old one had been dismantled at the LWL-Klinik, the search for an alternative location began after some time.
Finally, the majority spoke out in favour of the fire brigade car park.
However, this idea was not implemented - too many factors apparently spoke against a construction at this point.
At the end of June followed a U-turn.
The members of the Main and Financial Committee voted unanimously in favour of the Gutenberg-Schule area.
Jürgen Kohne says on the state of affairs that the administration is currently in the middle of concrete planning.
Then have the last saw the city council.
He expects a decision this year, so that it could be built with a positive vote in 2019.
Bottleneck in air traffic control gets even worse
Frankfurt/Hamburg According to the European air traffic chaos in the summer, German air traffic controllers see no signs of improvement in the coming year.
"This year we had wind, in 2019 we will have strong wind and then comes the storm," Matthias Maas, head of the air traffic control union, told the German press agency ahead of the aviation summit on October 5 in Hamburg.
An important reason for the numerous delays and flight cancellations is the long-known personnel shortage in European air traffic controls, for which there is no quick solution.
"The airlines and the airports can achieve rapid improvements for the coming year, but the training of new pilots lasts four to five years," the unionist said.
Across Europe, the market for ready-to-educated pilots is empty-swept.
Around a hundred people were missing in the centre of Karlsruhe alone to supervise the upper German airspace.
Maas again criticized that various aviation unions were not invited to the summit.
Representatives of the federal government, the states, air traffic control, airlines and airports will come together in the Hanseatic city on Friday to address the causes of the delays.
"If you want to talk about additional compaction and overtime, it won't be possible without us," explained the head of the GdF, which has many members at Germany's own German air traffic control.
Maas once again blamed requirements of the EU Commission for the pilot plight.
The traffic forecasts for the regulatory period running until 2019 were far too low.
The resulting cost requirements and personnel savings at the national air traffic controls are now fully impacting the operational business.
Maas appealed to Federal Transport Minister Andreas Scheuer (CSU) to campaign in Brussels for regulations that "don't cut off the airs even further."
At present, the EU regulations will be coordinated by 2024 and further intensifications are to be feared.
The EU is subject to constant lobbying pressure on the part of airlines.
"Ryanair CEO Michael O'Leary and his colleagues are now reaping exactly what they've sown themselves in recent years," Maas said.
He hoped that the high cost of delay among the airlines initiated a rethinking.
The GdF will only participate in the improvement of the situation if the German air traffic control (DFS) offers a "voicing training and future pact," Maas said.
The previously announced admission of 120 pilot pupils annually over the next five years is just enough to compensate for the age-related departures.
The necessary personnel development is not yet associated with this.
Thyssenkrupp Supervisory Board votes in favour of group restructuring
The members of the Thyssenkrupp Supervisory Board voted unanimously in favour of plans to restructure the Group.
According to Thyssenkrupp's interim chief Guido Kerkhoff, who was appointed chairman of the board at Sunday's meeting, the crippling group is to be divided into two independent companies.
Both companies are said to be based in Essen and listed on the stock exchange.
There should be no additional reductions in personnel beyond the already known cuts in the administration.
After two resignations, the supervisory body counts only eight heads on the capital side.
This is contrasted with ten representatives of the employees.
However, the final decision is expected to be made by an annual general meeting in 2020.
Among other things, the new Thyssenkrupp Materials AG is expected to contain the 50 percent share from the merged steel business with Indian partner Tata.
In the second Thyssenkrupp Industrials AG company, the business is to be bundled with elevators and supplies.
Barcelona: Violence at demos of policemen and Catalan independence supporters.
There have been violent clashes with numerous casualties in two simultaneous demonstrations by Spanish police and Catalan pro-independencies in Barcelona.
According to the city administration, about 3000 police officers from all over Spain gathered on Saturday when about 6000 independence supporters also demonstrated.
The Catalan police tried with a large contingent to keep the two protest trains away from each other.
According to the emergency forces, however, independence supporters repeatedly tried to break the security barrier.
They would have attacked the officers with paint and throwing bullets.
According to the information, there were wranglings also between the participants of the various protest trains.
According to rescue workers, 24 people were injured.
Police said there had been six arrests.
Better salaries and appreciation of colleagues
With their demonstration, the polices had wanted to pay tribute to colleagues who, almost exactly a year ago, acted against the independence referendum banned by the Spanish central government in Catalonia.
At the same time, they demanded better salaries.
Relatives of the Spanish police and the Guardia Civil find it unfair that Catalan and Basque police officers earn significantly more than they do.
The demonstrators perceived it as a provocation that the Spanish policians demonstrated in Barcelona of all places.
"Why did they come here to protest?" 55-year-old Catalan protester Manel Perez asked AFP.
Just to provoke and celebrate the fact that they threshed us a year ago.
A 31-year-old police officer from the Balearic Islands, who only wanted to give his name with Daniel, rejected the accusation.
"Of course we want to support our comrades, who last year only did their duty: defend the law," he said.
But the demonstration is there to enforce our rights and we're doing it in Barcelona because that's a big European city and there's more echo here.
However, he and his colleagues "did not come to provoke," the police officer assured.
