Musikkabarett: Gender understanding with heart - Wolbeck - Westfälische Nachrichten
"Fitting both for adults and for men" was the musical cabaret with Tina Teubner on Friday evening in the hall of the Christuskirche in Wolbeck.
As a cabaret, Teubner pre-empted gender understanding, not only with humour and warmth of heart for the (felt) woman and (felt) man and what else may exist, but also with music.
The mini-guitar used it and also mastered her violin.
Ben Süverkrüp sat on the wing.
On the one hand, he is the male sparring partner, who sometimes also speaks until the end of the movement, on the other hand a true pianist: So congenially harmonises Süverkrüp Teubner that some may have wished for a solo.
This also existed, with Beethoven and Chopin, but differently, namely the way it matched the red thread of the evening.
Teubner had brought a paradox into play: The satisfaction with the man comes when he has a partner, but does not open the door.
A closed door without a partner, on the other hand, brings no benefit.
The piano solo now demonstrates the same thing: what would have sprung from the composing of Mozart had "the Nannerl" opened the door unexpectedly?
Süverkrüp glides over in improvisations, from Bach's "Heach full of blood and wounds" into the "Song of Death," and out ofrom Mozart G minor symphony into a playful dance.
There was great applause and "Bravo" from the full hall.
Karlheinz Kleintje, treasurer of the association, handed over flowers to the chansonette.
But Chairwoman Heidemarie Markenbeck did not give any additional thanks.
She had the idea to treat the duo to appearances in Stuttgart and Frankfurt but also one in Wolbeck.
