International: Turkish community: Mosque opening as missed opportunity
The Turkish community in Germany (TGD) has criticized the manner of the inauguration of the great Ditib Mosque in Cologne by Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdogan.
"The mosque opening in Cologne has left a shambles in the German-Turkish relationship that can only painstakingly be swerved together," Gekay Sofuoglu, chairman of the TGD, told Germany's editorial network.
Both the Turkish and German sides would have missed the chance for a joint, conciliatory performance.
"Instead of underlining the affility of Muslims to Germany side-by-side with top German politicians, Erdogan used the date for his purposes," Sofuoglu lamented.
The extensive exclusion of the German public had "encouraged many reservations."
Heimatverein Nottuln: Offer has already become established
After the long summer break, it's finally folk meeting time again.
On October 18 (Thursday) at 19.30 the Heimatverein Nottuln already organizes its fifth Nottulner Folktreff in the fireplace room of the Alte Amtmannei.
Almost to the day, it’s been a year since we did a folk meeting for the first time.
In the meantime, this event has become an integral part of the musical calendar of events in Nottuln," says Hanns Moormann, chairman of the Nottulner Heimatverein.
I didn’t think that our folk meeting was established so quickly in Nottuln.
Whether Sturmtief Friederike in January or heat wave in July, the fireplace room of Amtmannei has always been fully occupied.
"As always, the program at the next Folktreff is a colorful mixture of German folk, hiking and sailor songs, folk songs, evergreens and old hitters," says Karl-Heinz Stevermüer, who repeatedly invites friendly musicians to Nottuln.
Klaus Spellmeyer agreed with his accordion for the first Folktreff "Birthday".
For many years he was a member of "Gaitling", a group from the neighboring district of Steinfurt, which played music with Low German lyrics.
Now he's out with an Irish-Folk band.
The creators of the Folktreff like to remember the last event in the summer, when Christoph Schulz from Folktreff Emsdetten delighted the listeners with his international version of the song "Frogs on the Night" and sent them home with an earworm.
"We reckon with many cheerful guests to spend a sociable and humorous evening again," reads the invitation of the Heimatverein.
Anyone who plays an instrument and wants to get actively involved is invited to participate.
Since the coveted seats are always occupied quickly, it is advisable to be there early enough.
The folk meeting starts at 19.30, but admission is already at 7 p.m.
