From pure fruit growing to ecology
With a gala in the Dr.-Stammberger-Halle, the 125th anniversary of the district association for horticulture and state care was celebrated.
On September 29, 1893, with a fruit show in the Kulmbacher Vereinshaus, the last founding impulses for the district association for horticulture and state care Kulmbach were set, and exactly 125 years later he celebrated a big anniversary in the Kulmbacher Stadthalle on Saturday evening.
Of course, at that time it was still called differently: As "Bezirksobsbauverein Kulmbach Stadt und Land" it was founded by Mayor Wilhelm Flessa in 1893.
Even the purpose of the fruit-growing association differed from today's work - it was actually purely about fruit growing, no one thought about flowers and horticulture at that time.
"At that time, in the epoch of the Enlightenment, the fruit tree was a favorite child of the elites," explained Günter Dippold in his festive lecture.
Fruit trees were supposed to line trunk roads, at schools so-called industrial gardens were created.
Fruit cultivation is still the task of the 55 horticultural associations in the district of Kulmbach, which comprise almost 7000 members.
But the spectrum is far from exhausted.
Near-natural horticulture, organic plant protection, village beautification and nutrition play an equally important role, such as imparting knowledge, a healthy diet and youth work.
The members of the Hutschdorf youth group "Maulwürfe" showed how versatile the tasks of a horticultural association are entertaining on stage.
"Youth work is urgently needed, especially in the digital age," said district chairman Günter Reif, and OB Henry Schramm also emphasized the importance of experiences in nature for life.
Collecting chestnuts was a huge thing for us children, I doubt that you can collect a lot of joy with smartphone & Co.
Schramm promised to plant two trees together with district administrator Klaus Peter Söllner for the anniversary.
"Where they are supposed to stand, the district association decides," Landrat Söllner added and emphasized the special importance of horticultural associations for the villages.
The district association stands for ecology, the beauty of the garden and green areas in the village.
That this is a real concern of the many horticultural associations in the district, the county managing director and specialist consultant Friedhelm Haun left no doubt.
He guided the many visitors through the long history of the district association with a picture lecture and reported, among other things, the need for self-sufficiency after the war.
Everything used to come out of the garden, for the winter it was grounded.
At the end of the 1950s, however, the utility garden turned into an ornamental and residential garden.
"Nature endures, it was already there before us and we have to orient ourselves on it," pleaded the expert for fruit and walnut trees.
Therefore, there is the incentive for the horticultural associations not to let up, so that our homeland remains beautiful and liveable.
This also includes welcoming the new citizens to our associations, added the president of the Bavarian state association, Wolfram Vaitl.
"We have to stop the blatant decline in insects, that's what we owe our children and grandchildren," concluded Günter Reif, who was solemnly framed by the towers of the Musikverein Thurnau.
Daniel Huber won summer GP in Hinzenbach
The Salzburger won well ahead of Peier and Geiger with 245.7 points.
Daniel Huber has ensured a home victory on Sunday at the Summer Grand Prix of ski jumpers in Hinzenbach.
With 245.7 points, the Salzburger won well ahead of Switzerland's Kilian Peier (237.7) and German Karl Geiger (237.3).
World Cup record winner Gregor Schlierenzauer (236.2) finished fifth just behind Russian overall leader Yevgeny Klimov (236.3).
Clemens Aigner (7.) and Michael Hayböck (9.) also came into the top ten.
Double world champion Stefan Kraft landed in 17th place.
In the overall standings, Huber is fifth ahead of Wednesday's last competition in Klingenthal.
Ski jumping results Sommer-Grand-Prix in Hinzenbach (OÖ) from Sunday: 1. Daniel Huber (AUT) 245.7 points (88.0 m/89.0 m) - 2. Kilian Peier (SUI) 237.7 (91.0/86.5) - 3. Karl Geiger (GER) 237,3 (87.0/88,0) - 4.
