Nafta talks priority: Canada's foreign governor postpones U.N. speech
Mexico's government wants clarity on Nafta: they issue an ultimatum to both trading partners for an agreement.
Canada's foreign minister now gives absolute priority to the trade deal - and postpones her speech to the United Nations.
Due to the time pressure in the struggle for a new edition of the North American free trade agreement (Nafta) with the U.S. and Mexico, Canadian Foreign Minister Chrystia Freeland postponed her speech at the U.N
Instead of today on Saturday, the minister will not speak until Monday in front of the General Assembly of the United Nations, said her spokesman Adam Austen.
In the meantime, she's focused on Nafta's renegotiation.
Mexican Economy Minister Ildefonso Guajardo had said on Friday that the negotiators of Canada and the U.S. would have 48 hours to agree if Canada actually wanted to join the trade agreement already reached between his country and the US.
The U.S. and Mexico had agreed in August on a recasting of the Nafta regional free trade agreement.
U.S. President-elect Donald Trump hinted that Canada could be barred from Nafta in the future.
The talks about a new edition have been going on for over a year.
By Sunday evening (local time) they are now to be brought to a close, Trump's administration wants to submit the agreement to the U.S. Senate before October 1.
In the event of a failure of the negotiations, Mexico's President-elect Andres Manuel López Obrador had announced on Friday to seek a bilateral agreement with Canada.
