Protests against presidential candidate Bolsonaro
Injured in a knife attack, Brazilian presidential candidate Jair Bolsonaro has left the hospital.
The far-right ex-military wanted to fly to Río de Janeiro where he lives, according to a report of the daily newspaper "O Globo."
It is still unclear after his dismissal whether the 63-year-old will make public appearances in the South American country last week before the presidential election.
The politician had been attacked on the open streat in early September during a campaign event in southern Brazil.
Meanwhile, thousands of people took to the streets in several Brazilian cities to demonstrate against Bolsonaro.
Under the title "Ele não" (in German: "Der nicht"), women's associations in particular had called for protests against the ex-military.
Also on the social networks, the associations against Bolsonaros made racist and women-despising statements mobile.
Several thousand demonstrators were expected in São Paulo's economic metropolis.
In the capital Brasilia, the protests shuffled with a rally by the presidential candidate of the Brazilian Workers Party (PT), Fernando Haddad.
He took the place of ex-president Inácio Lula da Silva, who had been sentenced to twelve years imprisonment for corruption and is therefore not allowed to run.
Brazil's presidential election is October 7.
As ex-mayor of São Paulo, Haddad has so far been far behind the far-right candidate Bolsonaro in polls.
The "Trump of Brazil" has been involved in politics for a long time, but recently presents itself as an anti-system candidate.
If none of the candidates reach the absolute majority in the first round of the election, there will be a run-off election on 28 October.
Observers assume that Bolsonaro will not emerge successful from this - no matter who he competes against.
Bolsonaro is known for inciting against homosexuals and blacks, glorifying the military dictatorship (1964-1985).
Again and again he shocks with verbal derailings.
Another time, he said Lula's left-wing Worker Party supporters should be shot.
Europe reclaim Ryder Cup title
Saint-Quentin-en-Yvelines - Just an hour away from Disneyland, European golfers have written another Ryder Cup fairy tale just outside Paris.
Captain Thomas Björn's outsiding team dethroned in front of more than 150,000 spectators over the three days at Le Golf National with great punches and outstanding team spirit the favoured defending champion USA around a toothless Tiger Woods.
After a long illness: Danish rock legend dies
Danish rock icon Kim Larsen has died.
The singer died on Sunday at the age of 72 after a long illness, his producer Jörn Jeppesen said on Larsen's website.
Larsen was surrounded by his wife and his six children.
The musician had been undercaded for prostate cancer since the beginning of the year.
Larsen had become well-known with the band Gasolin, which was enormously successful in Scandinavia in the 1970s.
Their songs were influenced by international music greats such as Jimi Hendrix, Bob Dylan and the Beatles.
After disbanding the band in 1978, Larsen was successful as a solo artist.
He sold around three million records in Denmark.
His album "Midt om natten" from 1983 is the best-selling album in Denmark to date.
Danish Prime Minister Lars Lökke Rasmussen paid tribute to Larsen on Sunday in a statement as a "street boy" who was "against everything, but in everyone's hearts."
The head of government also recalled that monarchy opponent Larsen had appeared in 2010 on the 70th birthday of Danish Queen Margrethe II.
Rock night in the shoe factory: volume at compressed air hammer level
"Here we stand, take the bone saw and ignite a concert evening with the power of a tiger". Alone the band names of "Here We Stand", "Tiger Bonesaw" and "The Ignition" suggest such a circumscription of the rocky live music experience on Saturday evening in the hall of the civic center Schuhfabrik: pure hard rock from three bands friends.
After the sound check and just before the start of the concert, the musicians even make time for a group photo.
No trace of stage fright.
Afterwards, the rock protagonists from Ahlen and the surrounding area - all in conjunction with the booking agency "Mosquito" from Oelde - with their bands visibly visibly inspire the approximately 120 visitors of the "Mint Mosquito" rock night.
Even the quartet "Tiger Bonesaw" with frontman and guitarist Dennis Hadrika convinces at the start of the concert evening with the latest, unknown pieces from their own pen and the soon-to-be released CD.
In front of the stage, a harmonious and powerful interplay of the band is danced during the performance.
Although "entiture" shouts cannot be overheard, "Tiger Bonesaw" then leaves the stage to the trio "Here We Stand".
That hard rock is not the same as hard rock is demonstrated by guitarist Sascha Bregenhorn, Golo Meierhenrich on bass and drummer Kai Hölscher with their alternative sounds, sometimes reminiscent of classical rock of the 1970s.
According to the "High Energy Rock" of the "Tigers" a synthesizer is also used at "Here We Stand".
Not only the longing, downright ballading "Take Me Home" impresses with the change from melodic passages to the guitar-heavy rock riffs of the multi-layered songs.
The quartet "The Ignition" from Tönnishäuschen, meanwhile, has gained a special degree of recognition since it was founded in 2005.
Full of agility, the band presents their hard rock on Saturday.
The fact that "The Ignition" has a special stage experience with its performances in front of a large audience - recently documented in a film by Max Meis about the tour in China of 2017 - is obvious and unmistakable.
Singer and guitarist Tim Jungmann, in combination with the band, also sets his own accents in the only covered song of the evening, "Word Up" by Cameo from the 80s.
In addition to the felt even higher sound than before, guitarist Norbert Brinkmann delivers an acclaimed solo, whereby he runs through the audience.
Of course, a certain amount of volume is part oficially part of this at such a hard-rock concert: According to information from the mixing console team at the three-way concert of "Here We Stand", "Tiger Bonesaw" and "The Ignition" at about 98 decibels.
This is, for example, at compressed air hammer level, but is thus still well below the controls of the "Wacken-Fesitval", which "The Ignition" refers to on their website.
However, the Ahlen audience does not need a destracting 120 decibels in jet driving volume as usual with the annual metal position in the north of the republic.
After almost four hours, the three bands of the harder, rocky gait have long since convinced with their sovereign, virtuoso playing and their own compositions.
"Here we stand, took the bone saw and ignited a concert evening with the power of a tiger," all three groups, of course figuratively, can say at the end.
And above all enjoy the great applause of the visitors.
