Refugees rescued from Libyan prison thanks to video
Nigerian refugees, with the help of a mobile phone video, have pointed out their catastrophic detention conditions in Libya, thus enabling their rescue.
The group had filmed the recording in their cell in the coastal town of Sawija in July under danger of life and circulated online.
"They refuse to send us back," said a man in the video message.
We're suffering here, we're dying here.
The recording was sent to "France 24 Observers" website, whose editors alerted the International Organization for Migration (IOM).
Refugees were finally flown to Nigeria at the end of August.
"Without the video, we probably couldn't have returned to Nigeria," Efe Onyeka, who captured the video, told AFP news agency.
The 25-year-old had been arrested in Libya while trying to get across the Mediterranean.
With the video, according to his own statement, he put himself in danger of
One of his comrades-in-arms had been beaten almost to death by guards, Onyeka said.
In general, the prison conditions were catastrophic: "They beat us all with pipes and sticks."
They didn't give us anything to eat, we had to drink from the latrine.
The IOM organises the voluntary repatriation of refugees stranded in Libya.
About 2,700 migrants from Nigeria had been sent back to their homes this year using the programme, IOM's spokesperson in the West African countrie, Jorge Galindo said.
The case of Nigerians who have now been rescued is not exceptional in view of the poor detention conditions in Libya.
However, he has not yet heard of a call for help via mobile phone video.
International organizations have long criticized the conditions in the Libyan refugee camps as inhumane.
The North African state, dominated in large part by militias, is the main transit country for migrants en route from Africa to Europe.
When a teacher went to elementary school town on Sunday afternoon to prepare her lessons for Monday, she surprised a burglar.
At about 16:50, the teacher entered the building via the main entrance.
In the forehouse of the 1st floor, she saw a man fleeing immediately.
First through a door, then over the roof terrace and from there over a stairs to the ground floor or the forecourt.
The teacher notified the police, who started the investigation.
It is not yet possible to say whether things or money have been stolen.
Perpetrator description: about 16 years old, black, very short hair, slightly darker appearance, dark pants, dark T-shirt.
