German was trapped in his own car for days
A 75-year-old had to endure several days and nights in his car in a forest area in Middle Franconia aftended the car away from all forest paths and a tree blocked the car door.
Rescue workers accidentally discovered the car near a pasture on Saturday afternoon and freed the missing man since Tuesday night, police said on Sunday.
The 75-year-old from Pleinfeld was in good health despite the cold nights and the long time without care.
He said he'd look forward to his home.
The pensioner had been reported missing by his family after failing to return from a ride of his SUV across local meadows and fields on Tuesday.
Despite intensive searches, the man's whereabouts had remained unclear for days.
Laschet urges rule of law in Turkey
Cologne NRW Prime Minister Armin Laschet (CDU) has urged the rule of law in Turkey in conversation with Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdogan.
Relations between the two countries are "overshadowed," Laschet said on Saturday after a meeting with Erdogan at Cologne airport.
This mainly concerns waves of arrest, freedom of the press and religion.
He had therefore made it clear to Erdogan that if relations are to normalize in the future, if economic relations are to be deepened, the rule of law is a very important precondition.”
Laschet also addressed the cases of Germans who are imprisoned in Turkey.
He expressed the expectation that freedom of expression would be respected.
Erdogan again emphasized the independence of the Turkish judiciary.
The conversation between Laschet and Erdogan had been moved at short notice to a building of flight readiness of the Federal Ministry of Defense at Cologne Airport.
Originally it was supposed to take place at Schloss Wahn - but the castle owners had rejected this out of political conviction against Erdogan.
Erdogan traveled on to the opening of the Ditib Mosque in Cologne after the talks.
Laschet had canceled a joint appearance in the building.
But he said: "The Muslims who go to this mosque are also citizens of our country."
And we are also their point of contact - and not the Turkish president.
