While U.S
Foreign Minister Ri is calling for an accommodating
North Korean Foreign Minister Ri Yong Ho has accused the U.S. government at the U.N
"The recent standstill comes because the U.S. relies on coercive measures that are deadly for confidence-building," Ri said in New York.
Disarmament is unthinkable for North Korea in these circumstances.
"Without any trust in the U.S., there will be no confidence in our national security, and under such circumstances, there is no way that we're the first to disarm ourselves," North Korea's chief diplomat said.
That punipulitus measures could "force North Korea to the knees" is "a pipe dream of people who don't know us."
However, in order to build trust, his country has stopped its nuclear and missile tests and destroyed a nuclear test facility, Ri emphasized.
From the U.S. side, however, there have been no "corresponding reactions," the minister criticized.
Due to North Korea's nuclear and missile program, the U.N. Security Council had adopted sanctions against the internationally isolated country three times last year alone.
U.S. President Donald Trump and his Secretary of State Mike Pompeo urged the United Nations in recent days to strictly adhere to the punitive measures.
