That they were actually in a youth meeting, the many visitors of the house of the open door HoT got nothing: white blankets and flower decorations on the tables and a large buffet were waiting for the guests after the Thanksgiving service.
The young people of the HoT had conjured up the buffet with delicacies from different countries in the new kitchen.
The institution was financed by donations from the community, for which the young people wanted to thank each other with the food.
The start with the new facility was not exactly easy, as Henriette Westbuer, the head of HoT, remembers.
We had already filled our refrigerators with food and ingredients before the summer break and were well prepared for the big day.
But then on the first day after the summer break came the nasty surprise: A blackout had put the huge, two-piece refrigerator out of order, the food was spoiled and had to be disposed of.
"Now we had to start all over again: although we got a lot of support from friends and neighbors, but the children and teenagers wanted to make it alone," Westbuer said.
And that's how everyone tackled.
The result was impressive, the guests were thrilled.
"Chefs can't do it any better either," one visitor praised impressively, while Hokkaido pumpkin soup was served or guests were allowed to serve quiche, liver cheese or cakes.
"It's really great what the children and young people have put on their feet," emphasized Father Frank Beckmann, while magician Stephan Lammen caused astonishment with card tricks and other feats.
