Macedonians vote on new state name
In Macedonia today, around 1.8 million citizens are voting on whether to change their country’s name in North Macedonia.
With a yes, the nearly three-decade smoldering conflict with neighbour Greece could be resolved.
Athens had insisted on a name change because of its northern province of the same name, blocking any rapprochement of Macedonia to the West.
Voters therefore also decide on future Western integration.
The U.S. had announced that the country could quickly become a NATO member in the event of a positive outcome to the referendum.
Syrians in custody.
Refugees attacked three women.
A Syrian beated three pensioners brutally and injured them severely.
The 41-year-old asylum seeker had rioted at about 10 p.m. on Friday in the Fluchtgasse in Alsergrund.
When the police arrived, they spoke to the accused, who went after passers-by with clenched fists and tried to attack them, on his behavior.
As a result, the Syrian tried to beat and kick the officers.
A little later it turned out that the suspect had struck three elderly ladies (65, 67, 75) with his fists to the ground shortly before in Währinger Straße at a tram stop.
In addition, he kicked the trio with his feet.
The pensioners were partly seriously injured.
They suffered hematomas, bruises and concussions.
The asylum seeker was also seriously injured "for unknown cause," according to police.
He was taken to the hospital and continuously monitored during treatment.
SPD politician: Johannes Kahrs marries his friend
Almost exactly one year after the entry into force of the marriage for everyone, the SPD politician Johannes Kahrs has married his longtime friend Christoph Rohde.
The two said yes on Saturday in Hamburg.
A registered civil partnership was out of the question for them, Kahrs had previously told the "Bild" newspaper.
"If we get married, it's right." He's been with his partner for 25 years.
The 55-year-old Kahrs is fiscal spokesman of the SPD parliamentary group and one of the spokespersons of the Seeheimer district, the conservative SPD wing.
In the Bundestag debate on the opening of marriage for homosexuals, Kahrs had caused a stir with an anger speech on June 30, 2017.
For example, he had accused German Chancellor Angela Merkel (CDU) of having "stumbled" on the issue.
"That was your Schabowski moment," he said in reference to Günter Schabowski's accidental announcement of the GDR border opening.
Since October 1, 2017, same-sex couples can marry just like heterosexuals.
Previously, they had been able to register their civil partnership since 2001, but there was no equality with marriage, for example on adoption law.
