Switzerland launches electronic register for organ donors
The register is intended to facilitate organ donations and relieve relatives in stressful situations.
The new procedure doees not change the fact that organs may still only be removed with explicit consent, Swisstransplant emphasizes.
(dpa) Switzerland is launching an electronic register for organ donors this Monday.
This should facilitate organ donations and relieve relatives in stress situations.
People who want to make their organs available in the event of death can register there.
Your wishes can then be retrieved by Swisstransplant if necessary.
The new procedure doees not change the fact that bodies - as in Germany - can only be taken with explicit consent, Swisstransplant emphasizes.
It is expected that 15 percent of the Swiss have a donor card, says Franz Immer, director of the Swisstransplant Foundation.
But only in five percent of cases, a card is found at the decisive moment with possible donors.
In over 60 percent of the conversations, the relatives do not know the wish of the deceased.
"You then have to decide on behalf of the deceased, which is often very stressful," always said.
A similar registration system already exists in Great Britain.
In Germany, there are considerations to store information on the possible organ donation on the health insurance card, as the spokeswoman of the Federal Center for Health Enlightenment, Marita Völker-Albert says.
"A registry in which peoples can enter their desires - pro or contra organ donation - doesn't exist," she says.
Federal Minister of Health Jens Spahn has spoken out in favour of a rescheduling of organ donation.
According to his proposal, everyone would initially be considered an organ donor, unless he or relatives object.
Among other things, the Catholic Church rejects this.
14-year-old student is world champion in memory computing
The 14-year-old student Tomohiro Iseda from Japan is world champion in memorizing.
He prevailed over the weekend in Wolfsburg against 39 competitors.
