Farmers are allowed to start annual kink maintenance on Monday
Schleswig-Holstein's farmers are allowed to start annual kink care on Monday.
The "green ramparts", which are unique in Germany, have shaped the landscape of the forest-poor state for around 200 years.
The tree and scrubbers up to five meters wide are "forest substitutes" and for many animals and plants the last retreats.
Knicks combine livelihoods scattered as corridors, over which the exchange of organisms can still function, as the managing director of the Naturschutzbund Deutschland (NABU) in Schleswig-Holstein, Ingo Ludwichowski, said.
Knicks are living fences
The knicks were created on the basis of an order of the Danish King Christian VI in 1766, who ruled over Schleswig-Holstein.
The "living fences" were not only field boundary and livestock fences, they also provided the farmers with firewood and beams for their half-timbered houses.
From that time also comes his name "Knick": By "buckling" of the branches, the dense, intertwined delimitations could develop.
Today, around 45 000 kilometres of the hedgerows extend between the North Sea and the Baltic Sea and lend the image to a wood-rich, diversely structured landscape.
According to the NABU, they are now used by around 7000 animal species as replacement habitats.
In one kilometer of kink, up to 1800 different species could live and breed up to 30 pairs of birds.
However, the kink can only fulfill its ecologically important function if it is maintained accordingly in winter.
Otherwise, he stumbles into a light row of trees that, in addition to biodiversity, also loses the wind protection effect, Ludwichowski said.
According to the Ministry of Agriculture, kink care may only be operated outside the breeding season from October to March.
After tsunami on Sulawesi Indonesians talks about situation on the ground
In an interview with the RedaktionsNetzwerk Deutschland (RND), the local Radika Pinto describes the situation on site on the island of Sulawesi after the earthquakes and the tsunami:
Were you on site during the tsunami?
No, I was in Jakarta on the island of Java at that time.
When the earthquakes were, my wife called me, she was in Palu.
When I found out about the tsunami, I immediately went back to Sulawesi.
That's when I arrived on Sunday morning, because I still had to drive more than 20 hours after a flight, because the airport on Palu was closed at that time.
What's the situation on the island now?
The national government has reopened the airport so that relief supplies can also be flown in.
When I arrived, I saw the houses on the coast, which are all completely destroyed.
Our office of "World Vision" has also been destroyed, but we have provisionally furnished something where we have accommodated other people as well.
Where are the people living on Sulawesi now?
Many have ran onto surrounding hills where they are still waiting for fear of another tsunami and earthquake and where they do not have access to water and food.
Some stay in front of their homes, there's still aftershocks.
Because many grocery stores and also the gas stations are completely closed, people partially steal food and gasoline.
The civil service is completely shut down, the national government is helping out.
A prison has also been destroyed, the inmates are partially running free and the search for them is difficult.
There are still a lot of bodies lying around, many people have not yet been found and are missing.
How is the communication and provision of information on the island?
At the moment we have mobile phone receptions, but it didn't work before and we only got information via radio.
Many people are still looking for their children, neighbors and other people they don't know if they're living and where they're staying, because people haven't been at home during the earthquakes, but at work, for example.
The tsunami warning system didn't work properly.
Don't you have any information on this?
We don't know the reasons for this, but when the all-clear came, a lot of "Okay, no tsunami" thought - and then he came after all.
In other regions not hit by the tsunami, there has been misinformation that a tsunami is hitting the region and they've ran to the surrounding mountains.
Isn't an island like Sulawesi lying on the Pacific Ring of Fire prepared for earthquakes?
Yes, there are earthquakes every day, but this has been the worst.
We usually know how we are responding to earthquakes and also train evacuations.
But the entire infrastructure has collapsed, the earthquake has hit a lot of people.
