Prof. Dr. Aladin El-Mafaalani discusses integration: A discussion on an equal footing
In front of them is the author, Professor Aladin El-Mafaalani.
He once again accepted the invitation of Ernst Reiling and Lore Hauschild.
"For many years now, I have set myself the task of bringing interesting people to Greven," explains the founder and chairman of the "Reckenfelder Treff" - the association that cooperates with the "Cultural Policy" alliance when hosting the evening.
El-Mafaalani enters the reading very loosely, describes the writing process of the book, talks about how difficult it has been to explain the topic comprehensibly.
With a lot of gestures, tangible numbers and one or the other joke, he brings listeners closer to one of the most conflicted topics: migration and integration.
Why are conflicts increasingly arising in spite of successful integration?
The Syrian-born author makes use of a metaphor when answering the question.
At a table sits the German society, at the cat table the migrants of the first generation.
Their children grow up in Germany and want to go to the table.
The third generation of migrants was born in Germany, they have always been sitting at the big table and now want to have a say.
This leads to conflicts.
The decisive factor is therefore not whether one classifies oneself politically on the right or on the left, but: "Am I for or against an open society?", he emphasizes.
At the beginning rather subdued, then the discussion round is always lively.
And there's a need for speech.
An Afghan refugee reports from his perspective, two teachers tell how they see successful or unsuccessful integration among students.
El-Mafaalani is in front, not on stage, a sense of the discussion: she's on an equal footing.
